**Test claims reference was cut mid-string but reference has no string literal**

The reference has no string literal, so a change relaxing the unclosed-string check to compare the fix's leftover literal against the reference's would still flag and leave this test green. Give the reference a shared unterminated string so the string check is what the verdict rests on.



<details>
<summary>Prompt for LLM</summary>

````text
File libs/code-review/infrastructure/agents/engine/is-usable-fix.spec.ts, lines 136-140:

Test claims reference was cut mid-string but reference has no string literal

This test does not verify what its name claims. It says the unclosed string is 'still' flagged 'when the original was cut the same way', but existingCode `'if (a) {'` has no string literal — the original is cut mid-block, not mid-string. Because the reference carries no unterminated literal, a change that extends the new reference relaxation to the unclosed-string check (flag only when the fix's leftover literal differs from the reference's — the direct generalisation of bracketImbalance's signature comparison, i.e. the exact scope violation this test is named to forbid) would compare leftover `"abc` against nothing, still flag, and leave this test green; only a crude 'reference is cut ⇒ excuse everything' bug is caught. Give the reference a shared unterminated string so the string check is the gate under test: existingCode `'if (a) {\n  run("old'`, improvedCode `'if (a) {\n  run("new'` — the block's `|{(` signature is shared and excused there too, so the verdict can only come from the unclosed-string check: the current code still returns 'truncated', while a reference-relaxed or removed string check returns null and fails the test.

Suggested code:

it('still flags an unclosed string when the original was cut the same way', () => {
    const existingCode = 'if (a) {\n  run("old';
    const improvedCode = 'if (a) {\n  run("new';
    expect(checkFix(existingCode, improvedCode)).toBe('truncated');
});
````

</details>


